    ],
    "links": [],
    "chapters": [
      {
        "id": "models",
        "title": "GLM4 configuration and decoder composition",
        "claims": [
          {
            "text": "Glm4Config reads head and layer dimensions, requires normalization epsilon and intermediate width, accepts absent hidden_act but rejects a present value other than silu, defaults KV heads to query heads and tied embeddings to false, and derives a missing or zero head_dim as hidden_size // num_attention_heads.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_ae1bbfd8361e46898f3ad771b7d8b4cd",
              "call_63b00422ed69474c98a8cb27eb7953a2",
              "call_1b584a7952674089b3f163142aa1beb7"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Each block performs input RMSNorm \u2192 attention \u2192 post_self_attn_layernorm \u2192 residual addition, followed by post_attention_layernorm \u2192 gated MLP \u2192 post_mlp_layernorm \u2192 residual addition. TransformerBlock explicitly requests FP16 from both input norms; GLM4 requests FP32 from both post-norms and the MLP output, while retaining FP16 gate/up intermediates. Residual additions remain in place on x.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_ae1bbfd8361e46898f3ad771b7d8b4cd",
              "call_37fd8a400d2a467686c5f132e92530a1",
              "call_f024acb197be4c4688f255fd7f937778"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Attention maps separate q_proj, k_proj, v_proj and o_proj keys under each self_attn prefix. The gated MLP maps gate_proj/up_proj/down_proj and additionally supports gate_up_proj through contiguous gate-first, up-second slices. Its fused-key option prohibits intermediate slicing above the inherited 55,296-wide limit, documented as a practical single-GPU quantization limit.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_ae1bbfd8361e46898f3ad771b7d8b4cd",
              "call_0ec99ef30c1e4c8bbfd9d3f928fb2d93",
              "call_f024acb197be4c4688f255fd7f937778",
              "call_ed3407075ddc492388eb76e9faa46d0b",
              "call_24aacdb56f0549c49f92d1c06b91e212"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "GLM4 selects GPTJ rotary style through the existing settings reader. That reader preserves partial_rotary_factor and rope_theta; the default frequency builder uses dim = int(head_dim * partial_rotary_factor) and inverse frequencies theta^(-2j/dim). Attention calls the native-backed RoPE.apply path, not apply_torch.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_ae1bbfd8361e46898f3ad771b7d8b4cd",
              "call_63b00422ed69474c98a8cb27eb7953a2",
              "call_f5b16af16570431ea52e5fd5e7d290d0",
              "call_0ec99ef30c1e4c8bbfd9d3f928fb2d93"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "zai-org/GLM-4-32B-0414 declares hidden width 6144, 48 query heads, 2 KV heads, 61 layers and intermediate width 23040. Separately, zai-org/GLM-4-32B-Base-0414 declares the same values. zai-org/GLM-4-9B-0414 instead declares width 4096, 32 query heads, 2 KV heads, 40 layers and intermediate width 13696. All three declare the registered architecture, head_dim 128, SiLU, epsilon 1e-5, untied embeddings, rotary factor 0.5 and theta 10000; their explicit values agree with the constructor's configuration mapping.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_fd091dec118344b7aa20e540b933dc95",
              "call_c2bb36555c7549c590939b1f61bfa1d5",
              "call_e8de9896e873472aa272c9e97c8715d6",
              "call_ae1bbfd8361e46898f3ad771b7d8b4cd"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "A scalar transcription gives Q widths 6144 for each 32B model and 4096 for 9B, with K/V widths 256 for all three. Each has 64 rotary channels and 32 frequencies. Fused gate/up widths are 46080, 46080 and 27392 respectively; each intermediate width fits the inherited unsliced-MLP limit.",
            "basis": "execution",
            "evidence": [
              "call_add4e58dea604dcd9326a8161672f7d4"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The two 32B configurations disable attention_bias, whereas 9B enables it. Glm4Config does not branch on that flag: the shared Linear loader reads optional per-projection bias tensors. Consequently the flag difference alone is not evidence that the 9B bias is omitted.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_fd091dec118344b7aa20e540b933dc95",
              "call_c2bb36555c7549c590939b1f61bfa1d5",
              "call_e8de9896e873472aa272c9e97c8715d6",
              "call_ae1bbfd8361e46898f3ad771b7d8b4cd",
              "call_ed3407075ddc492388eb76e9faa46d0b"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "The surrounding model sequence is model.embed_tokens, indexed decoder blocks, FP16-output model.norm and lm_head. The output head can fall back to embedding weights when configured as tied. prepare_inputs stores the original IDs and delegates to existing attention preparation, whose default is uncached FlashAttention; cached preparation retains its existing cache-capacity, position and page-alignment requirements.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_ae1bbfd8361e46898f3ad771b7d8b4cd",
              "call_0ec99ef30c1e4c8bbfd9d3f928fb2d93"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "findings": [],
    "summary": "Adds Glm4ForCausalLM registration and a shared-module decoder with four norms per block, GPTJ rotary settings and selected FP32 branch outputs. All three indexed GLM4 configurations were inspected; runtime compatibility remains untested."
  },
